                {
                  "type": "quote",
                  "content": "Wherever your heart is, that is where you'll find your treasure.",
                  "author": "The Alchemist, in The Alchemist — vindicated by the epilogue",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\">\n                    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THE VINDICATED LINE</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Spoken in the desert, proven at the sycamore</text>\n                    <rect x=\"100\" y=\"210\" width=\"900\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.08)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n                    <text x=\"160\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"25\">\"Wherever your heart is, that is where</text>\n                    <text x=\"160\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"25\">you'll find your treasure.\"</text>\n                    <text x=\"940\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"19\">— The Alchemist</text>\n                    <rect x=\"150\" y=\"490\" width=\"800\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.1)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"548\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">SEMINAR NOTE</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"595\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">The line inverts on a second reading: Santiago's heart</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"630\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">was always at the church where he dreamed — and at</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"665\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Fatima's oasis, where he has promised to return.</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"703\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"17\">An allusion, too: Matthew 6:21, inverted — treasure follows heart</text>\n                </svg>",
                    "caption": "The maxim the circular structure exists to prove"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "quiz",
                  "question": "In the Hassidic source tale, Rabbi Eisik uses the treasure to build a house of prayer. Coelho's Santiago keeps the gold and plans his return to Fatima. Why do critics consider this change especially revealing?",
                  "options": [
                    {
                      "text": "It shows Coelho was unaware of the original tale's ending",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It converts the tale's final vector from communal (treasure as obligation to others) to individual (treasure as the self's reward) — encapsulating the novel's 1980s expressive individualism in a single substitution",
                      "correct": true
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It proves the novel is anti-religious, rejecting houses of prayer",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It was forced by Brazilian publishing conventions of the period",
                      "correct": false
                    }
                  ],
                  "explanation": "Coelho demonstrably knew the source (the chain runs through Buber and Borges, both of whom he has acknowledged), so the change is choice, not ignorance. In Buber, the discovery matters because of what it builds for the community — the tale ends facing outward. Coelho's ending faces inward: completed selfhood, private wealth, the beloved as dividend. The substitution — community out, self in — is the most economical summary of how the novel adapts folklore to late-twentieth-century spiritual individualism, where realizing one's Legend is 'a person's only real obligation.'",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\">\n                    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">What the treasure becomes</text>\n                    <rect x=\"80\" y=\"200\" width=\"440\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n                    <text x=\"300\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">BUBER'S RABBI</text>\n                    <text x=\"300\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Builds a house of prayer</text>\n                    <text x=\"300\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">treasure as obligation</text>\n                    <rect x=\"580\" y=\"200\" width=\"440\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n                    <text x=\"800\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">COELHO'S SHEPHERD</text>\n                    <text x=\"800\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Keeps the gold, returns to love</text>\n                    <text x=\"800\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">treasure as reward</text>\n                    <rect x=\"200\" y=\"500\" width=\"700\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"550\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"20\">Community out, self in —</text>\n                    <text x=\"550\" y=\"588\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">the adaptation's signature in one substitution</text>\n                </svg>",
                    "caption": "The single most revealing change from source to novel"
                  }
                }
